<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diodorus Siculus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diodorus Siculus was a Greek historian from Agyrium in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> who wrote the Bibliotheca historica, a universal history in forty books from mythical times to about 60 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1715">Fifteen</a> books survive complete and others in fragments. Because he drew closely on earlier writers whose works are lost, his history preserves much information, including his descriptions of the Celtiberians and other peoples of the West.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus.jpg" alt="Diodorus Siculus" width="320" height="333" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diodoro_siculo_-_storico_di_Agira.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
